Lunar Landscape Requirements
6.1 Physical Specifications

The lunar landscape should be approximately 8 ft × 8 ft and should have a minimum side wall height of 3 ft. The structure should be foldable into 4 ft × 4 ft sections to improve portability and storage. Surface features should be based on the actual lunar south pole crater formations where possible, and the terrain should include integrated lighting for both day and night operations.

6.2 Environmental Features

The landform should include a crater-within-crater design based on South Pole region topology. It should incorporate 3D printed rocks and surface features, scaled representations of lunar materials, and visual barriers that prevent the outside world from being visible. The design should be portable, easy to assemble and disassemble, and should mimic realistic lunar terrain conditions.

6.3 Surface and Mission Realism Requirements

The surface should include realistic crater depth and elevation variation, rock and boulder placement to challenge navigation, and terrain layouts that support different mission objectives. The environment should include a barrier or enclosure to prevent visible outside surroundings. A controlled lighting system should allow lunar day and night simulation while maintaining realism and mission immersion.

Robotic System Requirements
7.1 General Specifications

The project requires four specialized robots. These robots should be controlled with a Bluetooth-enabled remote system and should operate within a range of 100 meters from the HERA module. The robots should use standard and infrared camera systems with SD card storage. Power requirements will be defined during the planning phase, and the robots should be designed for stable performance across uneven terrain and mission conditions.

7.2 Robot Types and Functions

Transportation Robot

The transportation robot should have a payload capacity of 0.5–1.0 lb and should be able to carry a 10 cm³ cube. It should include a mobile platform with a stable base and should be able to move payloads over uneven terrain.

Manipulation Robot

The manipulation robot should include a lifting mechanism or arm, precision placement capability, and cube transfer functionality. It should be able to perform careful handling and placement tasks in the lunar environment.

Support Robots

The support robots will have specialized functions to be defined during planning. Their responsibilities should complement the primary units and provide support for reconnaissance, assistive transport, or sensor deployment.

Communication and Control Systems

The robots should use a Bluetooth control interface that is compatible with HERA systems. They should also have video feed storage and transmission capability, mission control monitoring, data logging, and analysis features. The system should include error handling and recovery protocols to support mission resilience.

Goal for Student Prototype Development (2025–2026 School Year)
8.1 Scaled Model Development

The project should include a 2 ft × 2 ft prototype landscape scaled to 1:4 of the final design. This prototype should integrate with NASA HUNCH program guidelines and support student team collaboration. The design should be modular so functionality can be tested before the full-scale build is developed.

8.2 Robot Prototype Development

The robot prototype should use Arduino-based control systems, scaled robot designs, testing and validation protocols, and documentation standards. Safety and reliability checks should be built into the development process.

8.3 Outline Mission Scenario Testing

Mission scenario testing should include day and night operation verification, communication system validation, emergency procedure testing, user interface evaluation, and validation of mission objectives.

