Special Issues and Design Considerations
One key addition would be a lunar landscape that allows participants to control robots and potentially place ArduSAT cubes or other payloads on the lunar surface. HERAnauts would retrieve those cubes or robots remotely while they are in their lodging and lab spaces.

The objective of this phase is to collect the requirements and scenarios that HERA facilitators may be interested in using in the simulation. This project will include the following major components:

Testing a single Arduino robot with a Bluetooth remote control and a camera Defining the visual scope so that the robots remain within the scenario and do not see the outside world Evaluating how lighting affects realism, survivability, and mission continuity Defining the initial scenario in which robots work inside a crater with defined physical limits Producing a final report and engineering drawings for phase two development

Visual Isolation and Earth Visibility

A critical issue is preventing the robot camera from seeing the outside environment. If the robot enters a crater or reaches a high point, the camera may show walls, ceiling, or the Earth’s sky, which would break the mission illusion. To avoid this, the project must include visual barriers, camera limitations, and environmental layout decisions that keep the system within the intended lunar scenario.

Terrain and Layout Planning

The actual landscape size may be 8 ft by 8 ft, but dimensions must be carefully considered. If a robot hits a rock, a divot, or a smaller crater, its camera view may change and reveal the outside environment. The project therefore requires a defined frame, a controlled backdrop, and physically planned locations for rocks, boulders, takeoff zones, and task areas so that the robot remains within the simulation.

Problem Statement
The facilitators of the NASA HERA program, with the mission beginning in January 2026, require more activities for HERAnauts to test challenges that astronauts encounter in space. This includes navigating to multiple locations on the Moon and creating a facility where robots can be driven across a lunar-style environment.

This creates a need for a realistic lunar simulation environment in which participants can control robots, complete mission tasks, and experience space-like conditions.

Objectives
Create an 8 ft by 8 ft collapsible environment that mimics a small segment of the Moon in the South Pole region near Shackleton Crater. Use robots that can carry a CubeSat into the lunar environment. Include appropriate boulders, stones, craters, and terrain features. Provide a way to manage a regolith-like surface and keep track of terrain as mission scenarios change. Isolate robot cameras so they do not see ceilings, walls, or other objects that violate the simulation. Enclose the environment in a structure that collapses for storage and transport.

Constraints
The 8 ft by 8 ft terrain must collapse and be easily stored or transported. Surface features and terrain elements must be repositionable for different mission scenarios. The environment must support varied layouts such as crater zones, rocky surfaces, and mining or recovery areas. The system must support day and night lighting changes in later mission phases. The HERAnauts will move between a virtual environment and a physical facility environment, so the robot control interface must remain intuitive and realistic. The robots may need to carry, transfer, or retrieve a CubeSat payload while remaining within the mission scenario.

Deliverables
The project will produce the following:

A summary of deliverables, including the lunar moonscape and up to four lunar robots Robots with different functionality that provide the capability to transport a CubeSat from the HERA facility over a lunar landscape A system for placing and removing the CubeSat at the end of the mission Visual control systems and computer-based monitoring for each robot A final report documenting requirements, design approaches, and testing recommendations

Final Summary
This project defines the planning requirements for a realistic lunar landscape and four-robot mission system to support HERA analog activities. The built environment and robots are intended to simulate lunar exploration, robot operation, cube transport, and mission realism in a portable and scalable format.

This planning phase provides the requirements and scenarios needed to guide prototype development during the 2025–2026 school year. Successful completion of this phase will provide the foundation for a fully designed, testable, and operational lunar environment for future HERA missions.

